/* Cigar Cafe — 上級編の英語本文 1/4（葉と品種・製造）。英語版専用。元: data/advanced*.js */
(function () {
  if (typeof ADVANCED_DATA === "undefined") return;
  var A = ADVANCED_DATA;

  A.cultivation = "【Shade cultivation (tapado)】\nThe cultivation of wrapper leaf is broadly divided into shade-grown and sun-grown. Shade cultivation covers the field with a canopy of thin cotton cloth (cheesecloth) or nylon netting, and this covering is called 'tapado' (a covering) in Spanish.\n【The effect of shade on the leaf】\nSoftening the direct sun makes the leaf thin, supple and elastic, with finer veins too. The colour finishes a pale gold with a creamy, mild flavour, giving a smooth wrapper that rolls easily. Connecticut Shade is the leading example.\n【The origins of shade cultivation】\nThis technique is said to have begun in earnest in the Connecticut River valley of the US around 1900; hand-sewn cheesecloth was used at first, and nylon netting is now the mainstay on many farms. The valley's shade fields, said to have covered some 20,000 acres at their peak, are reported to have shrunk in recent years to about 2,000 acres.\n【Ecuador's natural cover】\nIn Ecuador, permanent cloud cover softens the direct sun as a 'natural tapado'. Requiring no artificial canopy and yielding stable, comparatively inexpensive shade leaf all year round, it has become a major producer of Ecuadorian Connecticut and Sumatra.\n【Sun-grown cultivation】\nSun cultivation exposes the plant to direct sun with no canopy, and the leaf becomes thick, oily and deep in colour, with thick veins too. Sweetness, spice, a toasted note, leather and the like — the character inherent in that variety is amplified across the board, becoming more powerful and full-bodied.\n【Seed and seedbed】\nTobacco's minute seeds are germinated and raised in a seedbed (semillero) and transplanted to the main field after some 40–60 days. Because the colour and sheen of a wrapper and the character of the leaf depend heavily on the variety's genetics, the selection of seed that combines disease resistance with flavour is the starting point of cultivation.\n【Harvesting method 1: priming (picking by leaf position)】\nMost wrapper and filler is harvested by priming — picking a few mature leaves at a time from the bottom of the plant upwards. Starting with the Seco at the lower part, then Viso, and finally the fully ripe Ligero, the picking proceeds from bottom to top in several passes.\n【Harvesting method 2: stalk-cutting】\nFor Connecticut Broadleaf, San Andrés and the like, stalk-cutting is used, in which the whole plant is cut off at the root and left to wilt in the field. Stalk-cut leaf takes on a more earthy flavour than primed leaf and tends to require a longer curing period as well.\n【Curing (drying)】\nAfter harvest the leaf is hung in a curing barn, where the green chlorophyll breaks down and turns to yellow-brown. For maduro uses of Broadleaf, the colour and texture are said to be brought into order under temperatures reaching up to about 150°F and strict humidity control.\n【Fermentation (pilón)】\nAfter drying, the leaf is stacked into a pile called a pilón and ferments through moisture, heat and pressure. The internal temperature rises to about 110–130°F, and the enzymatic reactions break down the pungent ammonia and convert starch into sugar. The pile is restacked each time the temperature rises, said to be repeated some 8–12 times.\n【Maduro as a 'state'】\nMaduro is not a variety but a 'state' produced by fermenting longer and hotter. Meaning 'ripe' in Spanish, it draws the sugars to the surface, making the leaf dark and oily. Contrary to received wisdom, this darkening in fact works to make the taste mellower and sweeter.\n【The gradations of colour】\nChiefly by the length of fermentation, the leaf is graded — from lightest — as candela (double claro), claro, colorado claro, colorado, colorado maduro, maduro and oscuro. Fermentation time, however, is not the sole factor that determines colour.\n【Añejamiento (ageing)】\nOnce fermentation is finished, the leaf is gathered into bales (tercios) and rested in a warehouse, and over months to years of ageing its edges are smoothed and its aroma matures. This quiet interval is thought to integrate the flavour settled by fermentation still more mellowly.\n【Terroir】\nThe same variety takes on a different character with soil, altitude and climate. In Nicaragua, the black soil of Estelí produces powerful, rich leaf; the fertile red clay of Jalapa smooth, sweet and more aromatic leaf; rocky Condega earthy leaf; and the volcanic island of Ometepe rich, roasted leaf — the character of the origin greatly influences the finished result.\n【Veins and grades】\nAfter harvest the leaf is finely graded by colour, size, veins and the presence of blemishes. Thin, smooth, unblemished leaf with inconspicuous veins is chosen for wrapper, and the rest is passed to binder or filler, so even leaf from the same plant divides in use and value.";

  A.blendingPhilosophy = "【The four axes of design】\nThe blender designs body, strength, flavour and combustion — separate axes — through the combination of leaf position (priming) and variety. Strength and flavour do not necessarily coincide; the key is to regard them as independently adjustable.\n【The basic principle】\n'The lower the leaf, the lighter and better-burning; the higher, the stronger and harder to burn' is the framework. The higher a leaf, the more sun it takes and the more its oils, sugars and nicotine are concentrated, so it is stronger and richer but harder to burn. This gradient is used to reconcile taste and fire.\n【The division of roles by leaf position】\nThe roles are set out as 'Ligero = strength and body, Viso = complexity and consistency, Seco = delicacy and aroma, Volado = securing combustion'. Each priming is dialled in and out by proportion to approach the intended profile.\n【A guide to filler proportions】\nA typical filler is said to set Seco and Viso together at their largest proportion of around 50–60% as the foundation of combustion and aroma, add 20–40% Ligero to build up power, and adjust the burn with Volado as needed. The more Ligero, the heavier and more peppery, and the slower the burn.\n【The structure of rolling Ligero at the core】\nIt is an iron rule of construction to place the hard-to-burn Ligero at the centre of the filler bundle and wrap it in the readily burning Seco and Volado around it to keep the fire alive. Placing strong leaf on the outside makes the fire prone to going out and causes an uneven burn.\n【The roles of binder and wrapper】\nThe binder provides the framework that gathers the bundle and burns it cleanly, while the wrapper is the frame around the taste, governing the whole first impression. Small though its surface area is, the wrapper is thought to contribute greatly to aroma and finish.\n【Origin differences as variables】\nBecause the same variety takes on a different profile with its origin, the strength of Estelí, the aroma and sweetness of Jalapa, the earthiness of Condega, the richness of Ometepe and so on are combined as variables of taste. A design that mixes leaf from several origins rather than relying on a single one to add depth is also common.\n【The wrapper is the frame around the taste】\nThe wrapper sets the whole direction through the choice of variety and state of fermentation. Light-coloured shade types are creamy and light; Habano and Corojo give pepper and power; and the maduros of Broadleaf, San Andrés and Brazilian varieties give sweetness and richness, enveloping the character of the filler.\n【Working back from the target image】\nThe seasoned blender first fixes a finished image — 'a creamy morning smoke', 'a medium of cedar and baked pastry', 'a full body as heavy as espresso' — and works back from it to choose leaf position, variety, origin, binder and wrapper, adjusting through repeated trial rolls and smoking evaluations.";

  A.rollingTechniques = "【What bunching is】The stage of gathering the filler leaves with a binder to make a 'bunch' (the interior). Here the path of the smoke — the air channel — is decided, governing draw, combustion and the quality of the ash. The methods run, in order of difficulty, entubado > accordion > book > Lieberman (a semi-automatic device).\n【Entubado】A method of rolling each filler leaf into a thin tube (a scroll) before bundling them. The hollow of each tube becomes an independent air channel, so the smoke passes evenly through all the leaves, giving a high yield of smoke and a beautiful ash. It can hold as many as eight leaves while keeping a good draw. The most difficult method, with a torcedor limited to around 120–150 cigars a day, it is used in the very finest series.\n【Accordion】A method of folding each filler leaf concertina-fashion from the outside inwards, then layering and bundling them. Easier to master than entubado and more productive, it allows 200–250 cigars a day to be rolled. Able to secure an appropriate air channel, it is held to be a middle-of-the-road technique widely used across many premiums.\n【Book (taco roll)】A method of layering several filler leaves and folding them all at once, like a book or a taco. The simplest and fastest, but because the leaves are not shaped individually the airflow is inferior and tends to be somewhat uneven.\n【Lieberman】A method of rolling the bunch with a manual, semi-automatic device. Because it makes uniform bunches quickly, it is used for mid-grade mass production and is distinguished from fully hand-rolled work.\n【How the cap is fitted】Flag — the Cuban handwork of twisting the end of the wrapper and pasting it to the head. Flat cap — the traditional Cuban flat finish. Double / triple cap — a method of layering two or three small pieces, the triple being hard to peel and held to be proof of hand-rolling. Pigtail — a decorative twist of the end left like a pig's tail, which needs no cutting and can be twisted off.\n【Winding on the wrapper】The wrapper is wound spirally, gap-free, from the foot (the lighting end) towards the head, stretching the leaf slightly. The end is fixed with a vegetable gum, and finally the head is sealed with the cap to stop the bunch from unravelling. An even tension determines the straightness of the burn line and the look.\n【Draw and density】The quality of the roll bears directly on the heaviness of the draw. Too dense and the draw is heavy, the smoke scant, and bitterness and heat appear; too loose and it burns fast, hot and coarse. The seasoned torcedor brings each cigar within the proper range by the feel of the hand and by scales and a draw-testing machine.\n【Moulding after bunching】Once the bundled bunch has been wrapped in the binder, it is laid in a wooden mould and lightly pressed to bring it to the prescribed thickness and a true round (or square) section. It is left for several hours, turned every few tens of minutes, to stabilise the shape and density before the wrapper is applied.\n【The division of labour among torcedores】In fine factories, the bunch maker (bonchero) and the wrapper roller (rolero) are sometimes separated according to skill, while sometimes one person handles the whole process. In either case the dimensions, weight and draw are strictly set for each vitola (format) and inspected daily.";

  A.fillerTypes = "【Long filler (tripa larga)】The finest construction, with the filler leaves run whole and unfolded over the full length. The continuous air channel gives an even burn and a stable taste, and the ash holds firm. The basic form of a premium cigar, it takes cost and labour.\n【Short filler (picadura)】A construction using chopped offcuts (scrap leaf). Common in cheap machine-made cigars, it burns fast and is prone to getting hot, and the ash crumbles easily. Used in the budget range and in dry cigars.\n【Medium / mixed filler】An intermediate construction mixing short and medium-length leaf. With combustion characteristics between long and short, it is positioned as a compromise between cost and smoke.\n【Cuban sandwich】A construction that places short to medium chopped leaf at the centre and wraps and bundles it in full-length long-filler leaf. Because the scrap leaf is enveloped in long leaf like a hot dog in a bun, it is called a 'sandwich'. Hand-rolled yet cheap to make, and good marques exist among them.\n【The division of roles among filler leaves】The upper Ligero provides power, richness and a slow burn; the middle Viso complexity and an even burn; the lower Seco a delicate aroma and combustion support; and the lowest Volado ignition and the sustaining of the burn. Typically a blend is built mainly on Seco and Viso with 20–40% Ligero layered on top.\n【Totalmente a mano】totalmente a mano = the mark of 'entirely by hand'. It denotes the top mark for a long-filler cigar made 100% by hand with no machine used at all. A plain 'hecho a mano' also includes semi-hand-rolled cigars whose binder was rolled with a device or machine, so entirely-by-hand is held to be the higher grade. Habanos too makes the great majority by this entirely-by-hand method.\n【Why long filler is superior】Leaf run over the full length has a continuous air channel, so the smoke passes evenly through all the leaves, the flavour is stable, and the ash holds firm and lasts long. The short chopped leaf has a large surface area and burns fast and hot, so the two differ in the delicacy and the persistence of the taste.\n【Machine-rolled and hand-rolled】Machine-rolled cigars (dry / homemade types) use mainly short filler and can be made uniformly, in bulk and cheaply. Hand-rolled premiums make long filler and entirely manual work their creed, adding value through the layers of the blend and the quality of the combustion. The two differ fundamentally in use and price band.\n【Blend design】Filler usually combines two to four kinds of leaf, tuning by proportion the power of Ligero, the complexity of Viso, the aroma and combustion of Seco and the ignition of Volado. To reproduce the taste of the same marque year after year, blends are built while managing stock of each leaf by origin, position and year of ageing.\n【The relation of combustion and construction】Even with long filler, too tight a roll makes the draw heavy and the burn stall, while too loose a one burns fast and hot. Only when the leaf composition and packing density of the filler mesh with the permeability of the binder and wrapper is the designed taste and an even burn line achieved.";

  A.boxPress = "【Definition】An ordinary cigar is cylindrical (round). A box-press is made by packing the rolled round cigar tightly into a square wooden mould or box while it is still soft and applying pressure, bringing the section to a square shape. The air pockets are pressed out and the packing becomes dense.\n【Various theories of origin ① the transport-origin theory】The most widely told holds that when cigars were once packed tightly and gap-free into boxes in Cuba to fit in a greater number, the moist, soft cigars pressed against one another under the rocking of sea transport and the mutual pressure, and naturally took the shape of the box — a squared-off form. It is also said to have had the practical effect of preventing damage from rolling about in transit.\n【Various theories of origin ② the deliberate-shaping theory】The cigar historian Richard Carleton Hacker puts forward the theory that it began when firms such as Henry Clay deliberately shaped their product square to stop it rolling off the table. Which brand was first is not settled, and no single origin has become the established view.\n【Method ① box-press】The general method of packing the rolled cigars into a partitioned mould box and applying pressure for a set time to give a gentle edge. The corners are somewhat rounded.\n【Method ② trunk press】A method of clamping each cigar between wooden slats, applying pressure evenly to the four faces and rotating it, taking 30 minutes to 12 hours to bring out sharp, crisp corners. It takes labour and skill and finishes finely.\n【The difference in smoking】Because the square shape packs densely, it generally tends to burn slowly and cool, bringing out the taste rich and even, with a firm ash too. It also has the practical merit of not rolling in the hand. The round shape has more room for airflow, so the draw is lighter and it burns somewhat faster. This is not a matter of superiority but a difference of design philosophy, and is held to be a matter of choosing by preference.\n【The modern practice】Today not only Cuban but also non-Cuban brands adopt it widely, and all deliberately shape it with a mould box, wooden mould or slats as standard. In recent years the range of finishes has broadened too, from the trunk press that competes on the sharpness of the corners to the soft press with its four faces crisply brought out.\n【Appearance and variation between individuals】The rise of the corners changes with the strength of the press and the moisture content after rolling, so even the same marque shows slight differences from one cigar to the next. Rested long, the corners can round off a little and revert, which is held to be a sign that the press is a physical shaping.\n【The difference from figurados】A box-press is purely a shaping that squares the section, a concept distinct from the figurados (irregular shapes) such as the torpedo and perfecto, whose ends are drawn to a fine point. Shapes that combine the two, like a square-sectioned torpedo, also exist.\n【The timing of the shaping】The press is done just after rolling, while the cigar still holds moisture and is soft. Forcing a dry cigar would crack it, so the key is to mould it during the short window in which it is pliable. Ageing after shaping fixes the form and heightens the retention of the corners.\n【Choosing by preference】The enthusiast who prefers the square shape values the rich, even taste of the dense packing and the ease of handling of a cigar that does not roll on the desk. Those who prefer the round shape favour the light draw and the honest burn. Neither is superior, and the connoisseur's view is to enjoy the way the same blend smokes differently by shape.";

  A.plumeVsMold = "【The key point】The judgement the connoisseur is most careful over. Plume is a sign of good ageing and harmless; mould is harmful and spreads within the box. The two can be told apart by colour, texture and where they sit.\n【What plume (or bloom) is】On an oily, well-aged cigar, the oil within (tobacco oil) and the sugars seep to the surface and crystallise — a powdery, white to pale greyish-white deposit. Appearing on oily cigars rested long in an ideal, sealed environment such as a tube, it is held to be a badge of ageing.\n【What mould is】A fungus that arises from excess humidity (generally above 70%) or poor ventilation. Besides white it often shows a colour — blue-green, green, blue, yellow, grey and the like — and left alone it spreads to the neighbouring cigar and infests the whole box.\n【Telling them apart ① colour】Plume is white to pale greyish-white only. Mould often takes on a colour — blue-green, green, black and so on. Since there is white mould too, however, do not decide on colour alone.\n【Telling them apart ② texture】Plume is a flat, lying, fine crystal on the surface; magnified, a crystalline structure like sugar or salt can be seen. Mould is a three-dimensional, fluffy structure that stands up cottony or cobweb-like.\n【Telling them apart ③ the brush test】Plume, lightly brushed or rubbed, falls away like a powder and leaves no trace. Mould is hard to remove, and even when removed it leaves a stain.\n【Telling them apart ④ position】Plume appears only on the surface of the body and not on the foot (the cross-section at the lighting end). If there is a deposit on the foot or inside, suspect mould, and if the foot is affected it often cannot be saved.\n【What to do】Plume can be smoked once wiped off with a soft brush or the like. Mould should be isolated and the affected cigar removed the moment it is found, and the humidor's humidity and ventilation reviewed. Cleaning the interior and correcting the humidity prevents recurrence.\n【Telling them apart ⑤ smell】Plume is all but odourless. Mould often carries a damp, musty smell, and the smell on opening is an aid to judgement too. Since smell, however, is highly subjective, judge it overall together with colour, texture and position.\n【Why plume is rare】Because plume appears only when oily leaf is rested long in an ideal environment, the chances of actually encountering it are few. The great majority of white deposits on the market are mould, and it is held to be safe not to jump to calling it plume.\n【Prevention】Mould arises readily from humidity above 70%, stagnant air and overpacking. Keep the humidity at 65–70%, avoid direct contact with the humidification material, and now and then change the air to ensure ventilation. Keeping new cigars separate from existing stock when they are introduced and watching them for a while reduces the risk of infection.\n【Beware the misconception】The popular notion that 'a white deposit = plume, so no worry' is dangerous, for white mould exists too. When in doubt, check whether it leaves a stain when wiped, whether it has reached the foot, and whether it is three-dimensional or flat, and if in any doubt it is wise to isolate it and refrain from smoking.";

  A.agingChemistry = "【Overview】Even in a humidor (ideally 16–18°C / 65–70% humidity), a finished cigar continues its slow oxidation, esterification and redistribution of components. Research reports that during ageing numerous volatile components (heterocyclics, terpenoids, ketones, esters and so on) fluctuate, and that amino-acid breakdown, microbial conversion, the Maillard reaction and carotenoid breakdown products create the aroma.\n【The decay of pungent components】Volatile organic acids, aldehydes and residual ammonia are drawn off, and the 'edge and grassiness' of youth fade. Most of the ammonia smell is said to go within a few months, and almost all within one or two years.\n【Change in stages】The aroma components during ageing are reported to shift not uniformly but stage by stage. One study divides the ageing process into several phases and finds that many marker components show an increasing trend over time, corroborating a course in which sweetness and an aged aroma rise after the young harshness.\n【Esterification】Acids and alcohols combine to form esters, and sweet, fragrant aromas like honey, dried fruit and cedar increase. As ageing proceeds the composition of the esters changes and the layers of aroma grow.\n【The Maillard reaction】Even during ageing, reducing sugars accumulate to serve as substrate, and sugars and amino acids react slowly to produce components that give richness and depth to the smoke. The slow reaction over time is the source of the roundness.\n【Tannin polymerisation and marrying】Polyphenols (tannins) polymerise, softening the astringency and bitterness. At the same time the oils and moisture of the three layers — wrapper, binder and filler — interchange in a 'marrying', and the flavour integrates.\n【The sick period】From just after shipping to a few months on, the cigar enters a sick period (periodo de enfermedad), when the flavour can close up and turn ammonia-like. The rule of thumb is to avoid smoking during this time and wait for recovery through ventilation and rest. Depending on the marque it is said to come twice — first, and again six months later.\n【The window and the peak】After recovery, many premiums reach their first window in one to three years, and powerful marques are said to open up further at five to ten. In excessive long ageing, however, some individuals thin out in taste, and the peak is not uniform, depending on the marque and the storage.\n【The involvement of microbes】Research reports that even during ageing the microbes on the leaf surface slowly break down alkaloids and biomass and take part in the conversion of flavour components. Ageing is coming to be understood as a composite process involving not only pure chemical reactions but microbes, enzymes and oxidation.\n【The practice of storage】The ideal is a stable environment of 16–18°C and 65–70% relative humidity. Sudden swings of temperature and humidity, or repeated wetting and drying, invite cracking of the wrapper and a deterioration of flavour. Not overpacking, turning the cigars now and then, and not neglecting the maintenance of the humidification material are the premises of good ageing.\n【The influence of the ageing medium】A cedar box or the lining of a humidor transfers a trace of aroma and works as a humidity buffer too. Research has shown that differences in the ageing environment (medium, conditions) produce differences in the final sensory and chemical characteristics, and it may be said that the quality of the storage is itself part of the making of the taste.";

  A.grading = "【Sorting by colour — escogida】Because the appearance quality of the wrapper greatly governs the grade of the product, the escogedor (colour grader) matches the colours box by box. Traditionally the leaf is divided broadly into several colour families and then subdivided by shade into dozens of gradations (once more than 100 colour names, it is said).\n【The colour scale (light to dark)】The representative divisions are candela (double claro) → claro → colorado claro → colorado → colorado maduro → maduro → oscuro. The colour deepens roughly in this order, and the impression of richness, sweetness and power tends to strengthen too.\n【The character of each colour】Candela is a greenish colour, made by rapid heat-drying that keeps the chlorophyll. Claro is a pale golden brown, smooth and gentle. Colorado is a reddish brown at the centre, full-bodied but gentle in aroma. Maduro is a deep brown from long fermentation and ageing, prone to sweetness, and oscuro is the darkest, close to black, and powerful.\n【The factors that determine colour】Colour is held to be determined chiefly by the length and strength of fermentation, but that is not the whole of it — leaf position (priming), variety, and the methods of cultivation and curing also have an influence. The higher a leaf that has taken strong sun, the darker it tends to be.\n【The arrangement within the box】After sorting, it is traditional to lay the leaf in the box in order from darkest to lightest, presenting a good look on opening and a uniform sense of colour. Matching the colours within a single box also builds trust in the reproducibility of the taste.\n【Dressing and authenticity】The anilla (band) came into wide use in the 19th century to keep the fingers clean and to identify the marque. The box is of cedar, transferring aroma and working as a humidity buffer. Genuine Cuban product bears a factory code and date code on the underside of the box, a green warranty seal, a hologram, a 'Totalmente a mano' mark and so on, which serve as clues to judging authenticity.\n【The escogedor's work】Colour sorting is handwork relying on the trained eye, comparing leaf one by one, or rolled cigar one by one, under a constant light close to natural daylight. The sense for classifying subtle shades into dozens of gradations comes from accumulated experience and is held to be hard for a machine to replace.\n【The limits of the colour–taste correlation】Colour is a guide to taste but not absolute. Even the same maduro varies greatly in taste with variety, origin and blend, and dark does not necessarily mean strong. Colour is no more than an appearance grade, and care is needed over the point that the actual strength and flavour are determined by the blend as a whole.\n【The meaning of grading】Matching the colour and grading the appearance is a commercial essential that ensures the impression on opening the box and the uniformity of the product. Because the wrapper also accounts for a high proportion of manufacturing cost, its grade bears directly on the price band and the standing of the brand.\n【The peculiarity of candela】The greenish candela (double claro) is an exceptional colour made by drying rapidly with heat rather than the usual natural drying, deliberately keeping the green of the chlorophyll. Once fashionable in the US market, it is said to have a distinctive vegetal flavour, and is a good example of colour not being determined by degree of fermentation alone.";

  var VARIETALS = [
    { use: "Originally wrapper; now mainly filler",
      feature: "Spanish for 'native, or indigenous, seed', it is held to be the oldest original stock, reaching back to the Cuba of Columbus's arrival in the New World. It is the genetic mother of almost all Cuban seed lines, and Corojo too is said to have been selected from Criollo. Reddish-brown and medium-bodied, with a savouriness in which saltiness and sweetness sit together. The hybrid Criollo '98, bred for greater resistance to blue mould and the like, is today's mainstay. Grown under shade it also makes a fine wrapper, said to be more balanced than the sun-grown leaf and more subdued in pepper. It is now cultivated in Nicaragua, Honduras, Mexico and elsewhere." },
    { use: "Traditionally wrapper",
      feature: "Said to have been established as a wrapper-only variety in the 1930s–40s at the El Corojo plantation in Pinar del Río (Vuelta Abajo) in western Cuba, through selective breeding from Criollo. Extremely vulnerable to disease, Cuba effectively abandoned the cultivation of pure Corojo after a major blue-mould epidemic. It is now revived in growing regions such as the Jamastran valley of Honduras. Spicy with a clear peppery character, medium to full. The original is delicate, but modern Honduran leaf and the disease-resistant hybrid Corojo 99 stand out for the richness of their sweetness and heat." },
    { use: "Wrapper, filler",
      feature: "Not a single variety name but widely used as a collective term for 'leaf of Cuban-derived seed grown in other countries'. Against the backdrop of US trade restrictions on Cuba, today's Habano wrapper is grown mainly in Nicaragua and Central America. Earth, pepper and a sweet roasted aroma; clearly more powerful and full-flavoured than Connecticut. In the narrow sense it can also refer to the disease-resistant hybrid Habano 2000, explained in various accounts as a cross of Corojo with a disease-resistant variety." },
    { use: "Mainly wrapper",
      feature: "A line emblematic of the Connecticut River valley in the US, encompassing two distinct strains — the shade-grown Shade type and the sun-grown Broadleaf. The roots of the Shade type are said to lie in a cross with Sumatra seed, and shade cultivation began in earnest in the valley around 1900. Shade is a pale gold, creamy and mild, with fine, delicate veins. Broadleaf is a broad-leaved variety grown in the region from earlier still; sun-grown and stalk-cut, it becomes thick and oily, and with the rich sweetness of molasses and dark chocolate it is the classic choice for maduro." },
    { use: "Mainly maduro wrapper",
      feature: "A Cuban-derived Negro strain grown in the San Andrés valley of Veracruz state, Mexico. Volcanic soil, moist sea breezes, good water and a cool altitude produce excellent leaf. Thick and rich in natural oil, it is ideal for maduro fermentation after stalk-cutting and long curing. As a maduro it is very smooth, with aromas of dark chocolate, coffee, earth and pepper. Closely related dark varieties are said to have been grown since the days of the Maya and the Aztecs. The lighter Cubano strain has a strong peppery spice." },
    { use: "Historically filler / wrapper",
      feature: "A legendary original Cuban variety, meaning 'golden hair', that enjoyed great fame in the first half of the 20th century. Named for the golden colour of its leaf, it was all but deified for its strength, sweetness and complexity. Extremely vulnerable to blue mould and very low-yielding, it was effectively banned from cultivation in Cuba after the major epidemic of 1979–80 — a phantom variety. In recent years there have been attempts to revive it in small quantities in Costa Rica (Felipe Gregorio reintroduced it from ancestral seed in 2014), Peru, the Dominican Republic, Nicaragua and elsewhere." },
    { use: "Mainly filler",
      feature: "A seed originating from the Piloto district of Vuelta Abajo in western Cuba. It is said to have been carried to the Dominican Republic around 1962 by producers who went into exile at the time of the industry's nationalisation around 1960, with an anecdote that it travelled in envelopes packed with cotton. Grown in the Cibao valley (Santiago province) of the Dominican Republic, it became the backbone of the country's premium leaf. Powerful and towards full-bodied with accompanying spice, it is described as carrying notes of cinnamon, nuts, earth and a light vanilla." },
    { use: "Mainly filler, binder",
      feature: "Said to be the only truly native Dominican variety, a thin and delicate one. Less durable than Piloto, but prized for its good combustion, its complexity and the creamy note that softens a blend. Mild with a light floral aroma, it is often used as an adjusting element that rounds off a rough blend or aids combustion. Widely grown in the Cibao valley of the Dominican Republic, it is often paired with Piloto Cubano to provide the delicacy and combustion of the country's blends." },
    { use: "Mainly wrapper, binder",
      feature: "A shade-grown variety native to the island of Sumatra in Indonesia, thin and smooth with earth, cedar, pepper and a faint sweetness. From the 1990s Ecuador made use of its permanent cloud cover as a 'natural tapado' and introduced Sumatra seed, giving rise to the darker, richer Ecuadorian Sumatra. More complex in flavour than the original from its homeland, with grassy nuances, a light sweetness and gentle spice. It is also a parent in the crossing that produced the Connecticut Shade strain." },
    { use: "Mainly wrapper (fine marques)",
      feature: "Grown in Cameroon in West Africa and in the Central African Republic. Its origin is said to lie in Sumatra seed brought in by Dutch colonists in the early 20th century. Blond to reddish, its fine 'tooth' — small beads of oil ranged across the surface — is the most pronounced of any wrapper. Cinnamon, baked-pastry spice, cedar, cocoa and nuts, in a balance of sweetness and pepper. A rare, expensive wrapper of unstable supply." },
    { use: "Mainly maduro wrapper, filler",
      feature: "Mata Fina, from Bahia state, is comparatively mild and sweet, with notes of cedar, coffee and chocolate. Arapiraca, from Alagoas state, is darker and fuller-bodied, with the spicy power of cocoa, earth and pepper. Both, with their natural sweetness, are much valued as maduro wrappers, and are often paired with Connecticut Broadleaf and the like. Brazil is also one of the world's leading exporters of leaf tobacco." },
    { use: "Wrapper to filler, depending on the variety",
      feature: "Because the original varieties were vulnerable to blue mould and the like and low-yielding, Cuban research institutions developed this group of disease-resistant hybrids by crossing them with disease-resistant varieties. Retaining the flavour framework of the originals while securing yield and resistance, they have become the mainstay of modern wrappers not only in Cuba but across Central America. Habano 2000 is held to be of the Corojo line, with various accounts of its crossing partner." }
  ];
  if (A.varietals.length === VARIETALS.length) A.varietals.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, VARIETALS[i]); });

  var PRIMINGS = [
    { pos: "The very bottom of the plant", burn: "Burns very readily", strength: "The weakest",
      role: "The thin, light leaf of the lowest tier, which the sun reaches least. Its contribution to flavour is all but nil, but for its outstanding combustion it is used mainly to sustain and even out the burn. The 'burning foundation' of a blend, the unsung support that keeps the fire alive even when hard-to-burn upper leaves are mixed in." },
    { pos: "The lower to middle of the plant", burn: "Burns well", strength: "Weak to medium",
      role: "The bearer of 'scent and aroma' rather than strength. Thinner and paler than Ligero and Viso, with a delicate, restrained flavour. Adding nuance and aroma without dominating the blend while aiding combustion, it is the foundation leaf that makes up the largest proportion in many blends. It also serves to fine-tune the overall outline." },
    { pos: "The middle of the plant", burn: "Good", strength: "Medium to medium-full",
      role: "An intermediate leaf, thinner than Ligero and thicker than Seco. Stronger in flavour than Seco and gentler than Ligero, it is a core leaf that balances flavour and combustion while adding complexity and depth 'without dominating'. Burning more evenly than Ligero, it also helps secure a consistent burn, making it versatile in use." },
    { pos: "The upper part of the plant (harvested last)", burn: "Hard to burn", strength: "The strongest (bar Medio Tiempo)",
      role: "The thick leaf that takes the most direct sun and is most concentrated in resins, oils, sugars and nicotine. The core of body and strength; a cigar's strength is determined mainly by the proportion of Ligero in the filler. Being hard to burn, it is rolled into the centre of the filler bundle and kept alight by the lighter leaves around it. In Nicaragua, the leaf of Estelí is famed as a powerful Ligero." },
    { pos: "The very top of the plant (the crown)", burn: "Hard to burn", strength: "Extremely strong",
      role: "In the traditional Cuban classification, the crown-shaped leaf at the very top of the plant. It injects power and spice in tiny quantities. Depending on the system of classification it overlaps with the concepts of Ligero and Medio Tiempo, and its name and delineation differ from one producer to another." },
    { pos: "Above Ligero — tiny leaves that grow, rarely, at the very apex", burn: "The hardest to burn", strength: "The strongest of all primings",
      role: "An ultra-small, ultra-oily leaf that grows, rarely, above the Ligero; a super-rare leaf said to number only two to four per plant (sometimes none at all). Holding extremely high concentrations of oil, sugar and nicotine, it is added in tiny amounts to limited high-end marques to deepen the finish and the power. It is found on plants of the Cuban seed line." }
  ];
  if (A.primings.length === PRIMINGS.length) A.primings.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, PRIMINGS[i]); });

  var WRAPPERTYPES = [
    { flavor: "Pale gold. Earth, leather, vanilla and a light spice, creamy and mild. Fine, smooth veins that roll easily. A staple for a morning smoke or a light blend. Besides the native Connecticut River valley of the US, the cheaper and more uniform Ecuadorian Connecticut is also widely used." },
    { flavor: "A sun-grown, stalk-cut broad-leaved variety, the classic choice for maduro. The rich sweetness of molasses, dark chocolate, espresso and toasted cedar. Thick, oily and elastic, with thick veins. Its thickness makes it hard to roll, but it is much used in premium marques." },
    { flavor: "Cuban-derived seed grown in other countries (especially Nicaragua). Peppery spice, earth and a sweet roasted aroma. Large in body and strength, oriented towards full flavour. Sun-grown, it becomes deep in colour and oily, and often serves as the face of a powerful marque." },
    { flavor: "Thin and smooth. Earth, cedar, pepper and a faint sweetness. Neutral to slightly spicy. The native Indonesian leaf is mild; Ecuadorian Sumatra, under the natural cover of cloud, becomes darker and more complex, with a grassy aroma and a light sweetness added." },
    { flavor: "Blond to reddish, with a fine tooth of oil beads that is the most pronounced of any wrapper. Wood, an earthy toasted note, cinnamon, cocoa and nuts, in a balance of sweetness and pepper. Smooth and refined, but a fine, rare leaf of unstable supply." },
    { flavor: "Grown on the volcanic soil of Veracruz, Mexico. Made into a maduro it is very smooth, with aromas of dark chocolate, coffee, earth and pepper. Thick and oily, with an earthiness from stalk-cutting. The lighter Cubano strain has a strong peppery spice." },
    { flavor: "Spicy, powerful and clear in its peppery character. Reddish-brown, medium to full. The original Cuban strain is delicate, but modern Honduran leaf and the disease-resistant hybrid Corojo 99 stand out for the richness of their sweetness and heat." },
    { flavor: "Mata Fina (from Bahia) is mild to medium, with cedar, coffee and chocolate and a gentle sweetness. Arapiraca (from Alagoas) is darker and fuller-bodied, with cocoa, earth and pepper. Both, with their natural sweetness, are well suited to maduro wrappers." },
    { flavor: "A vivid green. A 'double claro' made by harvesting the leaf while immature and drying it rapidly with artificial heat to fix the chlorophyll. Green grass, hay and a faint sweetness, mild. Once fashionable in the US, it later yielded the leading role to claro." },
    { flavor: "Claro means 'light', a pale yellow-brown, light and mild, which once took over from candela as the star of gentle marques. Colorado is a reddish-brown at the centre of the colour scale, full-bodied but softer in aroma than the darker leaves. Both denote a stage of colour rather than a variety name." },
    { flavor: "The darkest end of the colour scale. Oscuro is close to black, with the rich sweetness and bitter aroma drawn out by long, hot fermentation, and coffee, dark chocolate and earth. A 'state' obtained through fermentation rather than a variety name, using thick, sturdy leaf." }
  ];
  if (A.wrapperTypes.length === WRAPPERTYPES.length) A.wrapperTypes.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, WRAPPERTYPES[i]); });

  var PROCESSSTEPS = [
    { step: "Harvest",
      what: "Pick the leaves of a plant from bottom to top, in several passes by leaf position (priming). Starting with the lower Volado, it proceeds up through Seco, Viso and Ligero. The higher the position, the more sun the leaf receives and the thicker, higher in nicotine and more powerful it becomes; the lower, the thinner, lighter and better-burning. Shade-grown leaf for wrappers is hand-picked one leaf at a time, avoiding blemishes, and the right moment to harvest is judged to within a few days by the leaf's ripeness. The picked leaves are bundled by position, and their origin dictates their role in the final blend (power, complexity, combustion).",
      chem: "Fresh leaf is 80–90% water, high in chlorophyll, grassy and unsmokable. Nicotine and starch are at close to their maximum, and sugar is still scarce. The higher the leaf, the higher its thickness, oil and alkaloids; the lower, the more fibrous and porous and the more readily it burns. The biochemical potential at this stage sets the upper limit of the flavour that the subsequent curing and fermentation can draw out. Picked too green, the taste is unripe; too late, over-ripe and lacking definition — so judging the ripeness of each position governs the quality of the later stages." },
    { step: "Curing (drying)",
      what: "The leaves are hung in a curing barn (casa de tabaco / curing barn) and air-cured naturally over about 45–60 days. Temperature and humidity are adjusted by opening and closing windows and doors, and the leaf colour shifts from green to yellow to brown. The barn is generally kept at around 21–26°C, and the key is to draw the moisture out evenly, without haste. Because uneven colour or rot cannot be recovered at later stages, delicate management continues while reading the weather. Most cigar leaf undergoes this ambient-temperature air-curing, which differs in the direction of its flavour formation from the heat-applied flue-curing used mainly for cigarettes.",
      chem: "Chlorophyll a and b are broken down by enzymes (chlorophyllase and others), draining the green and revealing the yellow of the carotenoids (β-carotene, lutein) that lay hidden. Amylase hydrolyses starch into reducing sugars, and proteins too break down to free amino acids. Some of the sugar is consumed by the leaf's respiration. Moisture falls to about 20%, and the foundation of the flavour precursors is set here." },
    { step: "Moistening & Stemming",
      what: "Because the cured leaf is hard and brittle, it is lightly moistened by spraying or in a damp room to restore its suppleness (casing). Next, by leaf type and grade, the main vein is removed by stemming (despalillo). For wrapper and binder the vein is carefully removed half a leaf at a time so as not to damage the leaf; for filler it is treated as far as needed. Over-moistening invites mould and over-drying tears, so evening out the moisture governs quality.",
      chem: "Though a physical stage that restores the fibres' plasticity by re-adding moisture, the moistening raises microbial and enzymatic activity once more, forming the groundwork for the fermentation and ageing to follow. The thick main vein is removed because it causes bitterness, harshness and a hard burn, leaving the soft flavour components of the leaf blade. Uneven moisture translates directly into uneven fermentation temperature later." },
    { step: "First Fermentation / Pilón",
      what: "The cured leaf is stacked by type and grade into a large pile (pilón, 1.5–2 tonnes). The interior ferments through the pressure of its own weight and natural self-heating, and once the core temperature reaches about 35–43°C (accounts vary; in practice an upper guide of around 110–115°F), the pile is broken down and the top and bottom swapped in repeated restacking (volteo). Not letting it exceed 60°C is the iron rule — beyond that the leaf scorches and the flavour is greatly harmed. A single pilón lasts several weeks.",
      chem: "Full fermentation by microbes and enzymes. As it heats, ammonia, water and sap are released, and starch is converted into sugar. Pungent nitrogenous matter and off-notes are drawn off, and the Maillard reaction brings browning and a toasty character. Nicotine too decreases in stages. Restacking prevents a runaway temperature while at the same time evening out the degree of fermentation across the whole pile to suppress unevenness of flavour. Research reports that the microbial community on the leaf surface is the leading player in fermentation, breaking down alkaloids and proteins along with enzymatic activity to create flavour precursors." },
    { step: "Second Fermentation",
      what: "The leaf that has finished the first fermentation is moistened again and restacked, and the higher the grade, the hotter and longer the second fermentation it undergoes. A 'bespoke fermentation' is carried out, varying temperature and duration by leaf type — long for the powerful Ligero, short for the light Volado. Maduro wrappers take this stage long and strong to deepen the colour. Many marques repeat several fermentations across the whole process.",
      chem: "Residual ammonia is driven off further and nicotine brought down to the target value. Oxidative polymerisation of polyphenols deepens the colour and rounds the taste, and complex aroma precursors are generated. The breakdown products of carotenoids and the reactions of amino acids and sugars add layers of aroma. The strength of fermentation is held to be one of the main factors determining wrapper colour (claro to oscuro). The hotter and longer it is taken, the further the Maillard reaction of sugars and amino acids proceeds, deepening the colour and sweetness; the deep brown of the maduros is made through the management of this stage." },
    { step: "Escogida",
      what: "The leaf is sorted by use (wrapper / binder / filler = Ligero, Viso, Seco, Volado). Wrapper is finely sorted by shade of colour and graded by size, elasticity, fineness of vein and the presence of defects. The finest wrapper is chosen from leaf that is smooth, oily and inconspicuous in vein. Filler leaf too is divided by position, organised as stock that ensures the reproducibility of a blend.",
      chem: "Though a physical sorting stage, the leaf is lightly moistened to even out moisture and elasticity by grade. The accuracy of classification governs the uniformity of colour within a box and the reproducibility of a blend. Sorting accurately the differences in power, combustion and aroma that arise from leaf position (priming) is what makes a designed blend proportion produce the intended taste and burn. A misclassification here undermines the uniformity of the finished product. Because the physical properties of a wrapper — oil, fineness of vein, elasticity — bear directly on ease of rolling and combustion, qualitative indicators beyond colour are assessed at the same time." },
    { step: "Aging (Bulk)",
      what: "The sorted leaf is gathered into bales (tercio / paca) and rested in a cool warehouse for months to years. Premium leaf is aged 2–5 years, sometimes over 10. A slow, low-temperature fermentation smooths the edges into 'mellowness', and the more powerful the leaf, the longer the rest it needs. Chemical change proceeds extremely slowly even within the bale, and the leaf ages on as stock right up until the moment it is used in a blend.",
      chem: "Slow oxidation and esterification proceed. Pungent volatile acids and aldehydes decrease, and sweet, fragrant esters and aromatic compounds are formed. Tannins (polyphenols) polymerise, softening the mouthfeel. Reducing sugars accumulate to keep serving as the substrate for the Maillard reaction, and over time the integration and depth of the flavour grow. Because reactions proceed extremely slowly inside the low-temperature, low-oxygen bale, a smoothed-out maturity cannot be forced, and time itself becomes an element that makes the quality." },
    { step: "Rolling",
      what: "The torcedor moistens and evens out the leaf, gathers the filler with a binder to make a bunch, and presses it in a wooden mould. Next the wrapper is wound spirally from the foot up to the head, and the cap is fitted to finish. The weight and draw are checked for each cigar to bring it within the prescribed dimensions and density. The bunching method (entubado / accordion / book) changes the structure and the difficulty.",
      chem: "The stage that builds the physical structure of packing density and air channel. Too dense and the draw is heavy and bitterness appears; too loose and it burns fast and hot — the quality of combustion is all but decided here. Long filler secures a continuous air channel over the full length, producing an even burn and a firm ash. Slight differences of hand govern the smoke of each individual cigar." },
    { step: "Escaparate",
      what: "The rolled cigars are kept in the factory's ageing room (escaparate) at about 16–18°C and 65–70% humidity and rested for weeks to months. Excess moisture is drawn off, and the flavour disturbed by manufacture is settled before the cigars go to be boxed. Once the rest period set by each maker is met, they are taken out and proceed to the stages of colour sorting and dressing. Skip this and a young harshness remains.",
      chem: "The re-equilibration of moisture and the interchange of flavour components (oils, oleoresins) among the three layers of wrapper, binder and filler — the 'marrying' — proceed. The young harshness leaves, the burn steadies and the taste integrates. This is the decisive rest period in which the components settle from the tense state just after manufacture and the blend moves towards the harmony it was designed for." },
    { step: "Quality Control & Banding",
      what: "The cigars leaving the ageing room pass an inspection of dimensions, weight, appearance and draw, and are laid out in the box with the colours matched (traditionally arranged from the darker to the lighter side). Some factories carry out smoking tests on samples. The passed cigars are banded with the anilla (band), packed into a cedar box, and — for genuine Cuban product — marked with a factory code, date code, warranty seal and so on. The box is filled with the number of cigars for each format, sealed and shipped. Only here does it go out into the world as a product.",
      chem: "Chiefly a stage of physical and sensory inspection, but the cedar box transfers a trace of aroma and works as a buffer for humidity, setting the environment for the subsequent ageing in the shop and the home. A uniform draw and colour arrangement ensure the reproducibility of combustion and appearance, and set the premise that even after sealing an extremely slow ageing continues within the box." }
  ];
  if (A.processSteps.length === PROCESSSTEPS.length) A.processSteps.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, PROCESSSTEPS[i]); });
})();
